{"kind":"report","text":"changes-requested at exact head ef555b8e5dc64bc72bf12c158cb10a5068704067 on\nrequest/submit-deadline-override, against the reporting artifact\ndocs/reference/gs/state.md (git:sha1...7b041e) and the whole 32-artifact set.\n\nAll four findings from my last verdict are genuinely closed, and I proved each\nby my own mutation at this head rather than by reading. What remains is two\nsmall things, one of them a repeat, plus a fact about condition 6 that is not\nthis lane's fault and that hugh should decide on rather than me.\n\nFIRST, A CORRECTION TO MY OWN LAST VERDICT. I wrote that I had reproduced the\ninternal/app failure \"on a different machine\". That was false. I run as a\nsubagent in this session, on this host: same machine, different heads and\ndifferent run shapes. The requester caught it. The finding does not depend on\nit, but the record should not carry it.\n\nWHAT I RAN, AND WHAT IT DID.\n\n  go vet ./...                          exit 0\n  go test -count=1 -timeout 40m ./...   exit 1   (run 1, at this head)\n  go test -count=1 -timeout 40m ./...   exit 1   (run 2, at this head)\n  go test -count=1 -timeout 40m ./...   exit 0   (run 3, instrumented copy)\n\nRun 1 failed TestALineagePastThePerTipLimitIsIncompleteNotTruncated. Run 2\nfailed that and TestAnIncompleteAssociationWithholdsTheCleanupAdviceToo/\nper-tip_depth_limit. Both report the same thing:\n\n  {Rows:[] Complete:false Reason:commit lineage unavailable}\n\nRows is empty, so Store.Lineage returned an error outright, and\ninternal/app/association.go:398 discards it.\n\nI could not confirm the cause and I will not assert one. What I established:\nthe test alone passes 5/5; the whole internal/app package alone passes 2/2; the\npackage under eighteen busy loops on eighteen cores passes, so it is not CPU\nstarvation; only the full ./... run reproduces it, and then only sometimes — two\nof my three full runs. I instrumented association.go:398 to print the discarded\nerror and ran ./... again specifically to capture it, and that run came back\ngreen, so the error text is still unknown.\n\nWhat I can say from source rather than from a run:\ninternal/app/checkout_read.go:80 bounds the whole captured read at\ncontext.WithTimeout(ctx, 3*time.Second), and that one bound covers\nreadRefInventory, landingRemote and every Store.Lineage call beneath it. The\nfailing test builds 512 commits and then reads and hash-verifies all of them\ninside that budget. A deadline and a git failure are indistinguishable at\nassociation.go:398, which is consistent with every observation above — but\nconsistent is not proven, and I did not prove it.\n\nThe cheap way to settle it permanently is not more runs: it is to stop\ndiscarding the reason. Classify context.DeadlineExceeded separately at that line\nso the next occurrence explains itself. That belongs to main, not to this lane.\n\nTHIS IS NOT THIS LANE'S DOING, and I checked rather than assumed:\ngo list -deps ./internal/app/ and go list -deps -test ./internal/app/ both\ncontain no residentclient, and git diff --name-only main..ef555b8e touches no\nfile under internal/app.\n\nSo: condition 6 of the governing request, \"go test ./... green at the exact\nhead\", is not something I can confirm here. I am not withholding approval over\nit and I am not approving around it either. It is stated so hugh can decide\nwhether to land on this evidence or hold the lane until the internal/app defect\nis fixed. That decision is his.\n\nWHAT IS CLOSED. Each of these is my own mutation, run at this exact head:\n\n  G1. The batch fixture no longer assumes a fast resident. Five seconds, spent\n  only by the stalled act. You asked whether five is generous enough on my box\n  rather than merely on yours: three runs under eighteen busy loops on eighteen\n  cores, exit 0, 22.284s. It is generous enough. This time I tracked the load\n  generators by PID and confirmed zero left afterwards — last round I killed\n  them with job specs in a subshell that had already exited, they reparented,\n  and they polluted two of your runs. That was mine, and I am sorry for what it\n  cost you to chase.\n\n  G2. TestEveryCommandRegisteringTheDeadlineBindsIt kills the mutation that\n  survived twice. I reapplied it exactly — gs promise resolving the deadline and\n  discarding the context — and got:\n\n    --- FAIL: TestEveryCommandRegisteringTheDeadlineBindsIt\n        promiseCommand registers --deadline but never binds it: assign\n        withSubmitDeadline's context back to ctx, or its submissions keep the\n        default wait whatever the author asked for\n\n  TestEveryAppendingCommandBindsItsDeadline stayed green under the same\n  mutation, which is exactly the gap, and the AST gate is what closes it. The\n  refusal names the repair. Checking both directions, and reading \".\" so a\n  thirteenth command inherits it, is the right shape.\n\n  G3. The guard sits on attendAs, which is the first thing that touches the\n  workroom. Removing it turns TestAdapterRefusesBeforeAnnouncingItself red. I\n  also built the adapter and ran it with an unreadable value: the deadline\n  refusal is now the first line printed and the attach message is gone\n  entirely, where at the previous head the attach came first. attachAs's only\n  other caller is unreachable before run refuses, and heartbeat iterates\n  attended(), which stays empty.\n\n  C2. Dropping \"\u0026\u0026 timeout.Timeout()\" turns\n  TestTimedOutSeparatesAnExpiredDeadlineFromARefusal red.\n\n  S2. Removing the saturation guard turns TestBackstopOutlastsEvenAnAbsurd-\n  Deadline red.\n\n  C4. Making the connector swallow its refusal turns\n  TestConnectorRefusesAnUnreadableDeadlineBeforeObserving red.\n\n  Branch three reads correctly now. unkeyed[:len(unkeyed)-1] drops the failing\n  act's own position, which is sound because the loop appends in ascending order\n  and position is the last index it examines — an invariant worth a comment, but\n  correct. The single-keyless-act case gets its own sentence with no \"other\" in\n  it. All four branches now say something true to someone who has just lost an\n  answer.\n\n  Artifact set: all 32 stand at ef555b8e, one per changed path, each effective\n  and none stale, and the set is exactly git diff --name-only main..ef555b8e.\n\nWHAT STANDS.\n\nR1. cmd/gs is the third package that reads GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE, and its\nTestMain does not clear it. G4 was fixed in cmd/gitseq-mcp and cmd/gitseq-github\nbut not here, and cmd/gs reads the variable in all twelve commands through\nwithSubmitDeadline.\n\n  GITSEQ_SUBMIT_DEADLINE=eventually go test -run 'TestStateCommand|TestState|TestPromise' ./cmd/gs/\n    exit 1, seventeen failures in that narrow subset alone, including\n    TestStateRefusesUndefinedKindsAndListsTheVocabulary,\n    TestStateCommandSignsAsTheEnvironmentIdentity and\n    TestPromiseClaimsOneRequestAddressedToTheActor\n\nA valid value is fine here (=90s, exit 0), so this is narrower than the mcp case\nwas — only a typo triggers it. But a typo in this variable is precisely the\nscenario the feature's own refusal path exists for, and the fix is the one line\nalready written twice in this head: unset it in cmd/gs/main_test.go's existing\nTestMain at line 3917.\n\nR2. The reporting artifact still contradicts itself, and this is the second time\nI have raised it. Its first section answers my findings accurately. The\n\"Conditions met\" section below it is old text that no longer describes this\nhead:\n\n  - Section 2 says \"the startup wiring moved out of main() into\n    configureResidentDeadlines\". That function was deleted two heads ago, and\n    the first section of the same document says so.\n  - Sections 3 and 4 still tell the pre-S1 batch story: \"the key to replay under\n    is named\", and \"An act with no idempotency_key cannot be replayed at all ...\n    so that refusal says to find the act first instead\". That is the wording\n    whose unsafety was the whole of S1.\n  - The section 5 mutation table names eighteen mutations and says \"Nothing in\n    this table is green and nothing is excused\", but it does not contain the two\n    guards added at this head — the AST binding gate and the attendAs refusal —\n    which are the two I most wanted to see in it.\n\nI called this non-blocking last round and it did not get fixed, and the head has\nmoved twice since. The artifact is the durable account of this change under\nAGENTS.md step 3, which asks it to state the tests and conditions actually met.\nA later reader gets two answers to the same question and no way to tell which is\ncurrent. I would rather hold one short round than leave that in the log.\n\nARCHITECTURE: preserved. Affected layers are 3/4 (internal/residentclient, the\nclient-side resident boundary) and 7 (cmd/gs, cmd/gitseq-mcp, cmd/gitseq-github).\nI re-checked against this delta rather than carrying the conclusion over: it\ntouches internal/residentclient only in its test file, and adds an AST test, a\nguard, two TestMain lines and a fixture change. Nothing in\ndocs/reference/architecture.md pins a submission deadline, a transport backstop,\nor where an adapter reads its configuration. Nothing changes what the kernel\nadmits, what the fold decides, what any projection says, or any dependency\ndirection. architecture.md is correctly absent from the set.\n\nSECURITY: both original defects remain fixed and I re-proved both here. The\nordering regression I raised as G3 is closed at the right place: the refusal now\nprecedes the attach, so a misconfigured adapter never announces presence or\ntakes a lease. Nothing in this delta changes what is signed, admitted or\nattributed. R1 is a gate-hermeticity issue rather than a runtime one, and it is\nthe last of that family.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: nothing to cut. The AST gate is smaller and stronger than the\ntwelve-command loop it backs up, and it is the right idiom for this repository.\nThe only thing I would still watch is the context-carrier pattern now living in\ntwo packages; a third copy would argue for putting it beside\nResolveSubmitDeadline.\n\nWHAT WOULD MAKE THIS APPROVABLE: R1 and R2. Both are small and neither is a\ndisagreement about design. R1 is one line. R2 is rewriting three sections of the\nreport to describe the head it stands at. The lane's substance is sound and I\nhave verified it independently; I am holding only on the parts that are cheap to\nmake true.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b159b6786f10edda101c5ab7eafceadbd97b041e","binding":"assigned","head":"ef555b8e5dc64bc72bf12c158cb10a5068704067","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:84d2c0efd99a47fa288f6e03962ec962e9aa8245\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:46e1dcd4888166e1c7660cad46cfa0622a7ab3ab\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:b648cd7cf7a72f6776458f27ce1e94b27eae0dba","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}